  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe storms tonight, drier Monday, then a \nstrong cold front yields a chance at severe weather on Tuesday.\n\nCurrent water-vapor satellite imagery and the 00z upper-air maps\nfrom from the evening balloon launches depict a potent shortwave\ndiving through the region from NW to SE this evening. With a\nfair amount of instability still left (1000-1500 SBCAPE), this\nis sparking a few storms. With mid-level lapse rates in the 6-7\nC/km range, 30-40 kts of 0-6 km shear, and cold advection\noccurring at 500mb, a decent hail setup is taking place right\nnow. Central VA will see the best chance for severe hail, as \nshear drops off markedly to the NW, as does the instability. A \nfew severe thunderstorms have already occurred, and we could see\na few more before the night is done. Can't even rule out a few \n60 mph wind gusts with DCAPE values in the 600-800 J/kg range, \nwhich is sufficient for some severe downdrafts. \n\nWarmer and slightly more unstable conditions are expected \nMonday, but shortwave ridging aloft may limit thunderstorm \ncoverage. Overall, it looks like less coverage Monday afternoon \nthan today.\n\nA deepening shortwave-trough will drop from the eastern Great\nLakes into our area Tue afternoon pushing a strong cold front \nthrough the area Tue evening. Strong height falls coupled with \nstrengthening winds aloft will lead to greater thunderstorm \ncoverage and storm organization with a much greater risk of \nsevere thunderstorms. Damaging wind gusts appear to be primary \nrisk due to expected fast storm motions, but isolated tornadoes \ncan't never be ruled out in NW flow. Convection should start \nexiting the area quickly after 00Z Wed. \n\nOne caveat is potential for showers and thunderstorms early Tue\nmorning, which may leave behind plenty of clouds and limit \ninstability through the day Tue. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions return mid to late week.\n\nIn the wake of Tuesday's frontal boundary, the associated low will \nslowly meander towards the northeast over the next couple of days. \nThis should reduce rain chances overall, but spotty showers and \nthunderstorms remain possible as this low slowly slides offshore of \nNew England late in the week. High pressure moves in overhead \ntowards the end of the week bringing drier air and similar \nconditions overall as the week prior, with highs in the low to mid \n80s.\n\nHeading into next weekend, a broad area of low pressure begins to \nform over the Midwest and track eastward. As temperatures gradually \nrise towards more normal July averages, this could bring additional \nshower and thunderstorm chances to the region.",
